    <div class="azd-help-content">
        <section data-section="overview">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>The mission dashboard is on <strong>every Scene</strong> and shows the <strong>active squad</strong>: its
                members on the left, and the current objective, mission summary and key intel along the bottom. The map in
                the middle stays a normal, playable map. To see the Scene art without it, anyone can minimize it on their
                own screen.</p>
            <p>The dashboard only <em>shows</em> information that lives elsewhere, so it is always up to date:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Characters</strong>: their normal Actor and character sheet.</li>
                <li><strong>Mission text</strong>: ledgers, i.e. Journals in the <em>Mission Dashboard</em> Journal folder.
                    Ledgers are shared by all squads.</li>
                <li><strong>Which characters, People of Note and default ledgers</strong>: the active squad.</li>
                <li><strong>Reputation and resources</strong>: tracker Journals in <em>Mission Dashboard › Trackers</em>,
                    shared by every GM, squad and Scene.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="gm-quick-start">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-gear" inert></i> GM quick start</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Click <i class="fa-solid fa-gear" inert></i> in the dashboard header on any Scene (or right-click a Scene
                    and choose <strong>Configure Mission Dashboard</strong>).</li>
                <li>Under <strong>Squads</strong>, rename “Main Squad” or create more squads with <strong>New</strong>.
                    For each squad, pick its <strong>members</strong> (the <em>Player Characters</em> folder is listed
                    first), its <strong>default ledgers</strong> and any <strong>People of Note</strong>. Save.</li>
                <li>On each tab (objective, summary, intel), click <i class="fa-solid fa-book-medical" inert></i>
                    <strong>New ledger</strong> and name it after the operation. It is filed under the <em>Mission
                    Dashboard</em> Journal folder, can become the active squad's default, and opens its first entry for
                    writing. Untick <em>Players can read it</em> to prepare it privately.</li>
                <li>Switch the <strong>active squad</strong> with the dropdown at the top-left of the dashboard. It changes
                    for everyone and resets everyone's tabs to that squad's defaults, so you are asked to confirm.</li>
                <li>During play, use <i class="fa-solid fa-plus" inert></i> on a tab when the objective changes, and the
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-ranking-star" inert></i> Fame / <i class="fa-solid fa-coins" inert></i> credits
                    chips to record reputation and resources.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="azd-help-note">New Scenes need no setup. To hide the dashboard on one Scene (e.g. a title screen),
                untick <strong>Show dashboard on this Scene</strong> in its configuration, or right-click the Scene and
                choose <strong>Hide Mission Dashboard on this Scene</strong>. The module setting <em>Show mission dashboard
                on every Scene</em> switches the always-on behaviour off.</p>
        </section>

        <section data-section="player-quick-start">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-users" inert></i> Player quick start</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Your character's card updates by itself when wounds, strain, gear or critical injuries change on
                    the sheet.</li>
                <li>Click <i class="fa-solid fa-pen" inert></i> next to <strong>Desire</strong> on your card to write
                    what you'd like your character to pursue or experience. The GM reads these for plot hooks.</li>
                <li>Click a name to open the sheet. Click a portrait to view the art full size, or double-click it to open the sheet.
                    Click a critical injury to open its details. Orange chips under a name are powers the character is concentrating on (set on the sheet's Biotics / Tech tabs).</li>
                <li>Use ‹ and › on the mission panels to read earlier objectives and summaries.</li>
                <li>Need the whole map? Click <i class="fa-solid fa-eye-slash" inert></i>. It only hides the dashboard
                    on your screen, and the <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top brings it back.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="squads">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-people-group" inert></i> Squads</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>A squad is a group of characters with its own <strong>members</strong>, <strong>People of Note</strong>
                    and <strong>default ledgers</strong> for the three tabs. GMs can create as many as they like (e.g. a
                    ground team and a ship crew).</li>
                <li>One squad is <strong>active</strong> for everyone; its name is at the top-left of the dashboard. Only
                    GMs can change it (dropdown there, with a warning) or manage squads (configuration window).</li>
                <li>Changing the active squad sends <strong>everyone's</strong> tabs to that squad's default ledgers at
                    their newest entry. Changing a squad's default ledger does the same for that tab.</li>
                <li>Otherwise the dashboard remembers, per squad and in your browser, which ledger you last chose on each
                    tab.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="cards">
            <h2>Squad cards</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Wounds</strong> and <strong>Strain</strong> show the current value against the threshold,
                    as on the sheet. A value above the threshold is red and marked <em>over</em>. The bar stops at full,
                    but the number always shows the real value.</li>
                <li><strong>Soak</strong>, <strong>Def M</strong> (melee) and <strong>Def R</strong> (ranged) are the
                    sheet values, including gear and effects. A dash (—) means the value isn't set on the sheet.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical injuries</strong> are listed with their severity. Click one to open it.</li>
                <li>Cards always show the character's own Actor. Player-character tokens are normally linked, so damage
                    on them shows up. Damage on an <em>unlinked</em> token (a copy) doesn't.</li>
                <li>On small screens the cards become compact. Use <i class="fa-solid fa-expand" inert></i> on a card to
                    see everything.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="desire">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-pen" inert></i> Desire &amp; <i class="fa-solid fa-image" inert></i> art</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Desire</strong> is the same field as <em>Motivations → Desire</em> on the character sheet's
                    Basic Information tab; editing either one changes both. The character's owner and GMs can edit
                    it, up to 500 characters. If the sheet's Desire has formatting, you'll be offered the sheet
                    instead, because the card saves plain text. <strong>Save</strong> or Ctrl+Enter saves;
                    <strong>Cancel</strong> or Escape discards your changes.</li>
                <li>If someone else changes the Desire while you're typing, your text is kept. A warning shows their
                    version, and you can switch to it or save yours.</li>
                <li><strong>Full-body art</strong>: hover over the card art and click <i class="fa-solid fa-image" inert></i>.
                    Pick an image, or paste a path from your GM. The art fills the card's picture box, slightly
                    zoomed in and starting from the top; drag and zoom it to frame it differently. Leave the path
                    empty to go back to the portrait. If an image fails to load, the card falls back to the portrait.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="mission">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-crosshairs" inert></i> Mission panels</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Current objective</strong> (orange) answers “what do we do next?” Keep it short. It stays
                    visible even when the panels are collapsed.</li>
                <li><strong>Mission summary</strong> answers “why are we here?”</li>
                <li><strong>Key intel</strong> holds people, places and clues, with People of Note cards at the top.</li>
                <li>Everything is normal Journal content: links to Actors and Journals, images and formatting all work.
                    Secret blocks are only shown to GMs.</li>
                <li>GMs: <i class="fa-solid fa-pen-to-square" inert></i> opens the entry's editor. Changes appear for
                    everyone as soon as you save.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="history">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-book-open" inert></i> Ledgers &amp; entries</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Each tab shows a <strong>ledger</strong>: a Journal whose pages are that tab's entries. The newest
                    entry of the default ledger is what everyone sees first.</li>
                <li><strong>Switch ledgers</strong> with the dropdown under the tab title (★ marks the default), and
                    <strong>browse entries</strong> with ‹ 2/3 ›. Both only change your own screen; <strong>Back to
                    current</strong> returns to the newest entry.</li>
                <li>GMs: <i class="fa-solid fa-plus" inert></i> adds the next entry (e.g. <em>Objective 3</em>) to the
                    ledger you're viewing. It becomes the newest entry for everyone, and earlier entries stay as history.</li>
                <li>GMs: <i class="fa-solid fa-book-medical" inert></i> creates a new ledger, for example for a new
                    operation or a side job. <i class="fa-regular fa-star" inert></i> makes the ledger you're viewing the
                    active squad's default, which sends everyone's view of that tab to it. With no default set, the most
                    recently created ledger is shown.</li>
                <li>Ledgers live in <em>Mission Dashboard › Objectives / Mission Summaries / Key Intel</em> in the
                    Journal sidebar; rename, reorder or edit their pages there as usual. Your other Journals are never
                    touched.</li>
                <li>Players only see ledgers and entries they can read. A ledger created with <em>Players can read
                    it</em> unticked is hidden until you change its ownership.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="people">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-address-card" inert></i> People of Note</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>GMs add them in the configuration: choose the NPC's Actor, then fill in role, status, relationship and
                    a note. The name and portrait come from the Actor.</li>
                <li>A player only sees a person whose Actor they can see. Set the NPC's ownership to <strong>Limited</strong>
                    or higher (right-click the Actor, then Configure Ownership). Limited shows the name and portrait but
                    not the sheet. People players can't see are marked <strong>GM only</strong>.</li>
                <li>Everything typed into these fields is visible to players. Keep secrets in GM Notes.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="reputation">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-ranking-star" inert></i> Reputation &amp; <i class="fa-solid fa-coins" inert></i> resources</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Reputation</strong> tracks <strong>Fame</strong> (how well known the organization is) and how
                    much each <strong>faction</strong> likes them. It starts with the major Mass Effect factions.</li>
                <li><strong>Resources</strong> tracks <strong>Credits</strong> and raw materials (Element Zero, Platinum,
                    Palladium, Iridium, Aluminum…). GMs can add custom resources.</li>
                <li>Both are plain whole numbers with no caps or automatic effects. Open them with the Fame and credits
                    chips in the dashboard header. Everyone can see the totals and the history.</li>
                <li>GMs: use − / + on a row, set the amount, and give a <strong>reason</strong> (players can read it).
                    Fix mistakes with <i class="fa-solid fa-rotate-left" inert></i> <strong>Correct</strong>, which adds
                    a reversing entry. Archiving hides an entry but keeps its history.</li>
                <li>Both live in <em>Mission Dashboard › Trackers</em> and are shared by every GM, squad and Scene; two
                    GMs adjusting at the same moment both get counted.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="screen">
            <h2>Your screen</h2>
            <p>These only change <strong>your</strong> screen, never anyone else's:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-eye-slash" inert></i> <strong>Focus map</strong>: hides the dashboard. The
                    <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top of the screen brings it back. You can also bind a key
                    to <em>Toggle mission dashboard</em> in Configure Controls.</li>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-crop-simple" inert></i> <strong>Fit Scene</strong>: zooms and pans your view so
                    the whole Scene image sits inside the dashboard frame. GMs can make a Scene do this automatically
                    when it opens (<em>Fit into the frame when opened</em> in its configuration).</li>
                <li><strong>Frame style</strong>: by default the squad column and mission bar form a solid frame around
                    the map. Prefer separate floating panels? Change <em>Mission dashboard style</em> in Configure
                    Settings.</li>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-table-list" inert></i> <strong>Compact cards</strong>: cycles between Auto,
                    On and Off. Auto turns compact mode on for small screens.</li>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down" inert></i> <strong>Collapse panels</strong>: shrinks the mission
                    panels to one bar that still shows the objective.</li>
                <li><strong>Tab widths</strong>: drag the line between Objective and Summary, or Summary and Intel, to
                    resize the tabs; double-click a line to make them equal again. Saved in this browser.</li>
                <li><strong>Card art</strong>: drag a character's art to choose which part shows; a plain click shows
                    the art full size and a double click opens the sheet. Zoom with the <i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass-plus" inert></i> /
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-magnifying-glass-minus" inert></i> buttons that appear on hover, or
                    Shift + mouse wheel over the art; <i class="fa-solid fa-arrows-rotate" inert></i> resets.
                    Saved in this browser, and reset when the art changes.</li>
                <li>Scrolling over the dashboard scrolls the dashboard; scrolling over the map zooms the map.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="permissions">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lock" inert></i> Who sees what</h2>
            <table class="azd-help-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Configure the dashboard, manage squads, create ledgers and entries</td><td>GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Change the active squad</td><td>GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Switch ledgers and browse entries</td><td>Everyone, for themselves</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>See a card's stats and Desire</td><td>Observer or higher on that character</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>See only name and art</td><td>Limited on that character</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Edit Desire or art</td><td>The character's owners and GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Read a mission panel</td><td>Anyone who can read that Journal page</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Record reputation or resource changes</td><td>GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Hide, compact or collapse</td><td>Everyone, for themselves</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section data-section="tips">
            <h2>Tips &amp; troubleshooting</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>A panel says “Classified” or “No briefing available”</strong>: the page isn't shared with
                    players yet. Change the ledger Journal's ownership (right-click it in the Journal sidebar).</li>
                <li><strong>A character card is missing for a player</strong>: empty slots are hidden. A card for a
                    deleted Actor shows as unavailable.</li>
                <li><strong>A Person of Note is missing for players</strong>: set that NPC's Actor to Limited.</li>
                <li><strong>The squad column is short</strong>: collapse the Scene navigation, or turn on compact cards.</li>
                <li><strong>The dashboard is gone</strong>: check for the <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top of
                    the screen (you may have minimized it), or ask a GM whether it's hidden on this Scene.</li>
                <li><strong>My tab jumped to a different ledger</strong>: a GM changed the active squad or that squad's
                    default ledger. Pick your ledger again from the tab's dropdown.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
